            "The stress analogy should be used as a disciplined prompt for analysis, not as proof. A disputed clause may be one contributing condition among many, but an analyst must show the pathway by which it affected conduct. The relevant evidence might include a contemporaneous interpretation, a government decision that relied on that interpretation, the absence or failure of an agreed review route, and a subsequent response that increased risk. If the record supports only the existence of competing readings, the claim should stop there rather than asserting that the clause caused a later crisis.",
