            "Implementation guidance should preserve the boundary between explaining a commitment and changing it. An administrative manual may assign reporting formats or contact points, but it should not silently add a substantive duty that the parties did not accept. Where experience reveals a gap, officials should use the amendment, joint-commission, or interpretive procedure available under the instrument and record the result. Informal practice may help explain how parties understand a clause, but it should not be misrepresented as a formally adopted amendment.",
            "A successor government also needs an intelligible record of what is binding, what is provisional, and what remains disputed. A handover file can identify the latest authenticated text, current implementation status, open notices, unresolved claims, and the authority responsible for the next decision. It should include enough provenance for an independent reviewer to reconstruct the sequence without relying on one negotiator's memory. This is an administrative safeguard, not a substitute for public legal requirements or the parties' own procedures.",
            "The same discipline applies to a digital record. A digest can demonstrate that a particular record differs from a later one, but it cannot prove that the original text was lawful, consensual, complete, or accurately translated. Custodians should preserve the human approvals, source documents, version history, and context necessary to interpret the digest. A reliable seal protects the integrity of a record; it does not certify the truth of every statement within it.",
